Thanks Richard, I see the distinction you point out here, and it is important, so my approach is not entirely satisfactory, but maybe still useful in relative terms.  Especially as I’ll be comparing these results with patrol data consisting of a similar measure – encounters per unit effort. I may also experiment with transect width and translating into density, just to see how it looks.

Thanks again,

Hannah

On Monday, May 13, 2013 3:49:10 PM UTC+1, Richard Chandler wrote:
> This is why it is better, in my opinion, to model density rather
> abundance in some unknown area. Since you don't have either fixed area
> plots or distance data, you can't really get at density, but you
> should be able to map the expected number of snares exposed to
> sampling, given some transect length and the pixel-level covariates.
> But note that this is different than the expected number of snares in
> some pixel of your raster.
>
> Richard
>
> On Mon, May 13, 2013 at 10:33 AM, Hannah OKelly <hok...@wcs.org> wrote:
> > Hi Marc,
> >
> > Yes, a measure of abundance per km walked was what I thought I was getting,
> > possibly more appropriate as we just did one pass through each site and so
> > effectively sampled only a minimal proportion of it. My difficulty comes
> > when trying to translate this into a map, because I am not sure how to
> > reconcile the scale of my estimates with that of my covariate data used for
> > prediction.
> >
> > Hannah
> >
> >
> >
> > On Friday, May 10, 2013 9:48:43 PM UTC+1, Kery Marc wrote:
> >>
> >> hi Richard and Hannah,
> >>
> >> I'm not sure why simply using log(length) as an offset on abundance should
> >> not be sensible ? This should give you an estimate of snare abundance per
> >> unit-length transect. But yes, we have used length in a slightly more
> >> complicated way as a proxy for the proportion of the nominal sample area
> >> covered (in Royle et al, Ecol. Mono., 2007), because our transects sampled a
> >> 1km2 quadrat in a highly sinuous way with frequent loops.
> >>
> >> Kind regards  --  Marc
> >>
> >> ________________________________________
> >> From: unma...@googlegroups.com [unma...@googlegroups.com] on behalf of
> >> Richard Chandler [richard....@gmail.com]
> >>
> >>
> >> Sent: 10 May 2013 14:25
> >> To: Unmarked package
> >> Subject: Re: [unmarked] mapping spatial predictions using pcount
> >>
> >> Hi Hannah,
> >>
> >> Maybe Marc and Andy can weigh in on what they think is the best
> >> option. I know that in the past they have used transect length in some
> >> way as an offset of the number of individuals exposed to sampling.
> >> That might be a good option, but I don't think they simply used
> >> offset(log(length)). One paper that proposes a nice modeling framework
> >> for this type of data is Royle et al (2011, MEE):
> >>
> >> http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.2041-210X.2011.00116.x/full
> >>
> >> but the model requires that the individuals (snares in your case) are
> >> marked. Perhaps it's something to consider if you continue sampling.
> >>
> >> Richard
> >>
> >> On Fri, May 10, 2013 at 6:22 AM, Hannah OKelly <hok...@wcs.org> wrote:
> >> > Many Thanks Richard,
> >> >
> >> > I was, in part, following Kéry et al. 2005, who also had transects
> >> > within
> >> > quadrants and used route length as an abundance covariate to account for
> >> > similar variation within effective sampled area. My transects were not
> >> > fixed
> >> > width and there is considerable variation in terms of visibility amongst
